WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.720
It's the best action buffet yet in The Mandalorian
Chapter 6, which also fills in just a touch

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.240
more of Mando's mysterious backstory.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.580
After the events of Chapter 5 on Tatooine,
Mando is still desperate for cash, and in

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.299
that desperation bites off almost more than
he can chew on a prison break mission.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:21.300
He is definitely the honorable man Qin suggests
he is, but that doesn't mean he can't engage

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.070
in a touch of double-crossing to keep himself
alive.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.840
In the episode's final moments, we realize
that Mando took the Republic distress signal

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.500
from the prison ship — after turning it
off to give himself all the time in the world

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.050
to lock up his would-be mercenary teammates
and escape — and secreted it into Qin's

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.800
belt to draw Republic fighters back to the
mercenary base and destroy the rest of the

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.800
lot.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.530
He's playing to win, because if he loses,
it's his life and the Child's that will be

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:45.530
lost.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.120
His clever escape has put him in the best
position he's been at since the start of the

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.120
series.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.530
His ship is working, Baby Yoda is safe, he
has money, and with all that comes a lot more

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.260
options for him to stay on the run and effectively
hidden.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.850
We are entering the endgame of this first
season, however, so rather than feeling encouraged,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.659
we're more inclined to worry about how far
and fast the fall will be before the climax

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:05.690
of the finale.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:10.580
For now, let's drill into the illuminating
bits of Chapter 6: juicy details about Mando's

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.580
youth.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.270
This episode fills in some of the wide blank
space that previously existed in Mando's background.

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.490
Before Chapter 6, we knew he was an orphan,
and that in the present era he was a card-carrying

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:21.970
Bounty Hunters' Guild member.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.790
Until he snagged Baby Yoda out from under
his client, we had no reason to believe he

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:30.590
was anything other than the honor-bound but
morally-grey gunslinger we first saw him as.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.180
Any orphan will have had a rough childhood
— and maybe even a rebellious phase of their

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.930
youth — but it seems young Mando was quite
the violent outlier.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:42.190
It's difficult to reconcile the sad single
dad we've come to know with the Mandalorian

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.579
this assortment of reckless murderers once
knew.

00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:48.960
In Chapter 4, when Mando is asked why he adheres
so strictly to the Mandalorian way of life

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.700
and never removes his helmet, he says he wants
to honor the people who have given him a life

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.700
to live.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.369
We've been presented with a whole new side
of that life in Chapter 6, and it seems there

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.579
was a time when Mando did not respect those
gifts at all, and used them for his own ego

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:03.189
and vanity.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:07.230
Mercenary commander Ranzar "Ran" Malk recounts
Mando's general attitude in their previous

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.450
partnership, when the young Mandalorian apparently
referred to the grittiest and most illegal

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.530
mercenary work as "target practice."

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.239
Xi'an also suggests Mando is growing soft
when she learns of Baby Yoda's presence on

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:20.750
the ship and Mando tries to play him off as
a pet.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.560
"Didn't take you for the type."

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.730
When released from his prison cell, Qin comments
that the man "who left [him] behind" has suddenly

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.409
reappeared as his savior.

00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:31.659
Just what "type" did Mando used to be?

00:02:31.659 --> 00:02:36.590
It makes sense: An orphan is recruited from
a very young age into a warrior society, trained

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:40.629
in various methods of lethal martial arts,
and graduates out into the galaxy with no

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:42.109
personal attachments.

00:02:42.109 --> 00:02:45.920
We know Mando hates droids as a result of
whatever part of the Clone Wars he lost his

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.249
family to, and any young man orphaned by violence
could understandably grow up very angry, and

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:55.709
when given the ability to show off as a badass,
pounce upon it to fill that void in his life.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:59.930
The deeper question yet to be answered, however,
is what exactly happened to change Mando's

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.010
outlook and reorient himself correctly within
the Mandalorian warrior ideal as a controlled,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.169
merciful, and honorable man?

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:11.099
When did he stop using his gifts for murder,
get out from under an illegal mercenary squad,

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:12.719
and join a guild?

00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:16.529
Whatever it was and whenever it happened,
it stuck, because Mando spends this entire

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:20.139
episode trying to work around his teammates'
bloodthirsty impulses.

00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:24.209
By the end of the episode, Mando frankly has
every reason in the galaxy to kill each one

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:28.269
of them for the sake of his own safety, and
the safety of Baby Yoda, not to mention how

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:31.279
completely they have betrayed his minuscule
amount of trust.

00:03:31.279 --> 00:03:35.499
He chooses not to off them because he's a
better, gentler, and more emotionally-centered

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:36.859
man than he used to be.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:40.379
We can't shake the feeling that this seems
more and more to be the very thing that will

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:44.540
undermine him as his past continues to catch
up with him in this vicious backwater corner

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.860
of the galaxy.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:49.569
The point of this series is not to tell us
how cool Mandalorians are — the point of

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:53.980
this series is to tell us how kind and good
this Mandalorian tries to be, despite the

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:55.059
world he lives in.

